WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.520
Picture this. Someone tries to trigger

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:06.760
you. They raise their voice, they try to

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.840
get a reaction out of you, and instead

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:13.240
of getting angry or defensive, you stay

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:16.960
so calm it makes them nervous. That's

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:19.280
real power. The power of not reacting. I

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.000
have seen it decide the outcomes of

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:24.840
negotiations, change power dynamics in

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.120
the boardroom, make or break careers.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.440
Power doesn't belong to the loudest

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.640
person in the room. It belongs to the

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.880
one who never loses their calm. So, in

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.400
this video, I'm going to show you how to

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.640
never lose your calm under pressure and

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.800
master the frameworks that let you stay

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:44.840
unshaken inside and let nothing affect

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.640
you. But, here's the first thing that

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:50.400
will surprise you. The harder you try to

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:53.520
stay calm, the more anxious you get.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.960
Most people misunderstand calm. They

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.640
think calm is something you force, like

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:00.080
flipping a switch. You know, the

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:03.080
psychologist Daniel Wegner ran an

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.440
experiment. He told people, "Whatever

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.560
you do, don't think of a white bear."

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.200
What happened? Everyone immediately

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.760
thought of a white bear. The command to

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.680
suppress something becomes the trigger

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:18.680
itself. He called it ironic process

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.440
theory. Calm works the same way. The

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.440
moment you tell yourself, "Don't panic,"

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.680
your brain [music]

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.680
grabs the word panic and starts

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:30.480
panicking. And now, suppression becomes

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.920
an amplifier. And why does stress fire

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.040
in the first place? Science tells us

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.760
that when your brain observes that the

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.240
demands of the situation exceed the

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.280
resources it has, it triggers stress.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.160
It's not just what's happening, it's

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.960
whether you believe you can handle

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:50.360
what's happening. Here's what most

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.960
people miss. Calm isn't performed in the

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.920
moment, it's built before the moment.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.120
And calm also gives you a hidden

00:01:54.920 --> 00:02:00.080
advantage. Let's talk about that next.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:03.200
Staying calm is your biggest power move.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:06.400
Imagine you're the pilot of a commercial

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:09.920
flight. Early morning, half the cabin is

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.440
half asleep. Then, bang. You know, birds

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.840
slam into both engines. Both engines

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.720
fail. The plane starts dropping. What do

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:20.120
you do next? That was the exact

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.959
situation Captain Sully Sullenberger was

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.200
facing. He didn't have much time at all.

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:27.280
In that moment, air traffic control

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.760
didn't matter. The checklist didn't

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.080
matter. What mattered was his own

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.880
emotional temperature. He had to choose

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.640
to land the plane on the Hudson River.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:40.200
And 90 seconds before the impact, he got

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:43.280
on the intercom and spoke in a very calm

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:45.959
voice that was direct and professional.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:48.840
No shaking, no tremble, no panic hiding

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:51.840
between the words because he knew the

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:54.240
cabin would mirror his state. He stayed

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.640
very composed. He landed the plane on

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.800
the river and saved 155 lives. Sully

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:01.840
controlled himself first before

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.440
controlling the plane. That's the

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.840
sequence that matters because he knew a

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.160
vital truth. In a crisis, [music]

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:12.720
people don't follow your words. They

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:14.640
mirror your state. A leader who panics

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.920
spreads panic. A leader who stays

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.720
composed stabilizes the room. Now, let's

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.680
talk about the framework that builds

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:24.720
that kind of calm before the storm hits.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.519
The first framework is about creating a

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.519
gap where most people collapse it. I

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:32.720
call it the three spaces. I once asked

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:36.440
my music teacher how he could take

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:39.160
dramatic risks on stage, improvising

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:41.480
insane rhythmic combinations in real

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:44.160
time on the fly. And he said, "Well,

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:46.640
when I'm on stage, I'm not threatened."

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.000
His answer was less about music and more

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.400
about his mindset. [music]

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.840
He wasn't making choices to protect his

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.147
ego. He was just letting go. When we're

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.720
threatened, our stress level rises.

00:03:55.147 --> 00:03:58.200
[music]

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:00.480
Our brain gets flooded with high level

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:03.080
of stress hormones. Our brain switches

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.560
focus from the CEO part of the brain,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:08.560
which is the prefrontal cortex, to the

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:10.920
amygdala, your alarm system. And you

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.200
stop thinking like a CEO and start

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:16.160
reacting on instinct. So, if you want to

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.680
stay calm under pressure, then your CEO

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.760
function has to still remain online,

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:25.080
right? How? You create three spaces that

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:28.280
most people never find. Space one is

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:31.080
between stimulus and response. Victor

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:34.200
Frankl survived the concentration camp

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:36.413
by living in this very gap, the space

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:39.040
between what happened to him and how he

00:04:36.413 --> 00:04:41.760
[music] chose to respond. That space is

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.360
very tiny. Something happens, most

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.480
people react instantly, but that space

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.640
is everything. If you can separate

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.520
what's happening to you from what you do

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:54.480
next, you stay in control. Space two is

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.720
between urgency and importance. Stress

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.040
tends to compress time. When you're

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.320
under pressure and when the cortisol

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.720
level is high, you'll want to react

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.640
urgently. You'll be more prone to

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.543
interrupting others, rushing into

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:06.720
decisions,

00:05:06.543 --> 00:05:08.880
>> [music]

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.320
>> thinking in black and white, and

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.640
overreacting in general. So, you have to

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:16.800
create that space and [music] you do

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:19.280
that by slowing down the reaction time

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.040
and by separating what feels urgent from

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.800
what actually is important. And finally,

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:27.720
space three is between identity and

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:30.120
outcome. This is the hardest gap. When

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.680
your ego is fused to the outcome,

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:35.160
everything becomes personal. Any

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:38.040
disagreement feels like disrespect.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:41.320
Minor flaws feel like major failures.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:44.040
There is a study out of UCLA that found

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:46.560
that social rejection can activate the

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.640
same pain-related circuits in the brain.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.880
So, your brain treats any rejection or

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:54.560
an attack on your status just like a

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:56.440
knife wound. If you separate who you are

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.040
from what's happening to you, you'll

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:02.360
still remain calm every time the stakes

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:05.200
get high. But, we can only be calm when

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:07.960
our ego isn't on trial. Those are the

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.480
three spaces. So, how do you make this

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:13.120
actionable right now? The best and

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.440
fastest biological circuit breaker is

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.120
the physiological sigh. Because when you

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.400
stress, your breathing gets shallow,

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:22.440
carbon dioxide builds up, and your heart

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.000
rate climbs. You can't think your way

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.640
out of it, but you can breathe your way

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.960
out. Double inhale through the nose and

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:32.720
one long exhale through the mouth.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:35.200
That's physiological sigh. This is the

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:38.680
fastest reset to your nervous system. It

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:41.200
drops your physiological stress quickly.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.520
And that triggers a downshift for your

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.280
stress response. Knowing these gaps is

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.840
the foundation, but what do you do when

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.520
the pressure hits? You change the frame

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:51.640
before you change your feelings. You

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.080
know, Mike Tyson nailed it when he said,

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:56.120
"Everyone has a plan until they get

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:59.040
punched in the mouth. What matters is

00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:01.240
what you do next." On my first job back

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.560
in Boston, I was told to turn a

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:05.680
boardroom into a demo center. And I had

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:08.080
worked on it for weeks. We were showing

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:11.640
it to the CEO at 10:00 a.m. And when I

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:14.480
arrived at 9:30, my boss, Paul, was

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:17.480
bright red and fuming. "How could you

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:19.200
show up just 30 minutes before? Do you

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:21.680
understand this is your first

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.919
interaction with the company's CEO?" He

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:28.960
was raging mad. And I stayed quiet. I

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:31.919
apologized and I moved on. The CEO came,

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:34.320
the demos worked okay. Later, I realized

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:36.880
that I had the right context, but I

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.960
couldn't access it under pressure. I

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:41.720
didn't tell my boss that I had been in

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.880
that room until 4:30 a.m. in the morning

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:45.920
making sure everything was perfect,

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:47.840
everything was working, and that's the

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.960
problem. You always find the best

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:53.360
response later, when you're calm. So,

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:56.640
how do you stay present and not feel

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:59.840
threatened? I use a four-step framework

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:02.120
I call calm. Control the frame, absorb

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:05.200
the hit, lower the stakes, move

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:08.040
decisively. Calm. C is control the

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:10.120
frame. This is the most important first

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.960
step, because we all react to the same

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:15.280
moment in completely different ways,

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:18.360
right? Some escalate, some freeze, I go

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:21.160
quiet, but high performers ask before

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:24.360
reacting. What game is being played?

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:27.800
What is the actual stake? Is this threat

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:30.320
real or just loud? Most existential

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:33.360
threats you'll find are temporary

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:35.640
misalignments. So, control the frame by

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:38.360
naming what's actually at stake. Label

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:40.840
it. A absorb the hit. When pressure

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:43.360
hits, you may not prevent a first spike,

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:45.080
but you can control what happens next.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:48.000
This doesn't mean you take abuse

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:51.280
silently, but let it land without

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:54.486
returning fire. Then, reorient. See the

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:56.839
pressure building as raw data. Downshift

00:08:54.486 --> 00:08:59.480
[music] it internally. For instance, if

00:08:56.839 --> 00:09:01.600
someone comes at me nowadays, I reduce

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:03.800
it to physics. I think, "Well, these are

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.120
just sound waves, not a verdict." And

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:08.560
that allows me to focus on the message

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:10.640
and the intention without absorbing the

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.600
heat. It keeps me present. [music]

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:15.280
Because you have to give your brain

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:17.880
those few seconds to come back online.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:20.600
Third is L, lower the stakes. Remind

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:23.240
yourself this is a conversation or a

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:25.600
discussion or an argument. There's no

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:26.581
bear charging at you. This is not a

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:26.760
personal threat.

00:09:26.581 --> 00:09:29.240
>> [music]

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:31.040
>> So, that lowers the stakes. Just because

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:33.760
they are triggered doesn't mean you have

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.096
to be. I do this at home sometimes. I

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.160
misread a remark,

00:09:35.096 --> 00:09:38.040
>> [music]

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.840
>> make it personal, and withdraw. That's

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:43.000
not calm, that's me being

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.880
passive-aggressive. Remember to ask for

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.760
clarification before [music] making

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.520
assumptions. And you know, this matters

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:52.200
even more nowadays because our digital

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:55.680
life trains us to misread tone and

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:59.280
intention constantly. Finally, M move

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:02.280
decisively. Act once, clearly, without

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:04.560
emotional baggage, one clear action, one

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:07.191
clean sentence. When you finally make

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:07.240
your move, it should set the tone.

00:10:07.191 --> 00:10:09.480
>> [music]

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:12.080
>> When you react with compassion, with

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:14.720
calm, you can change the room. It's like

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:16.560
playing tennis. You served, the ball is

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:18.840
in their court. Now, they have to

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:21.240
respond to your calm. That's leverage.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:24.120
So, that's the framework. Calm is how

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:27.680
you stop being hijacked. Most people

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:29.640
react. High performers reframe. Control

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:32.920
the frame, control the room. And

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:35.080
finally, calm isn't a personality trait,

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:37.240
it's a survival skill because we're

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:39.920
living inside two storms that

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:41.880
destabilize us constantly. What are the

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:45.240
two storms that surround us at once

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:48.280
nowadays? First, the social media storm.

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:51.240
It's designed to keep us in a constant

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:54.000
state of rage and anxiety because

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:57.000
controversy pays better than calm. And

00:10:54.000 --> 00:11:00.200
second, the AI storm. And that one is

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:02.760
injecting real fear, real uncertainty,

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:05.120
and doubt in all of us about the very

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:08.720
future of work, about the very nature of

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:11.120
work. So, yes, staying calm is harder

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:14.200
nowadays than it used to be. But, high

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:16.520
performers learn to stay focused anyway.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:18.839
In 2008, for example, while markets were

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:21.680
melting down, Warren Buffett wrote in

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:24.960
The New York Times seven famous words,

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:27.000
"Bad news is an investor's best friend."

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:29.040
He stayed disciplined and looked for

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.160
opportunities where others saw

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.560
catastrophe. And here's what makes this

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:36.480
super hard. I knew his playbook. I

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:39.400
understood the math. I saw the signal. I

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:41.240
read that article. And I still sold at

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:43.800
the worst time, at the bottom of the

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:45.880
market. And I bought back at the peak.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:48.160
And it cost me real money. So, the good

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:50.560
news is that we can train ourselves to

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:53.800
weather these storms. The system can be

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:54.480
built. The bad news, it takes time and

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:56.920
deep [music]

00:11:54.480 --> 00:12:00.280
deep self-awareness. And the hardest

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:02.000
part is the ego. Separating who you are

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:05.160
from what's happening to you. The

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:07.280
question isn't how do I stay calm? It's

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:11.000
what am I trying to protect here? So, at

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:13.800
a deeper level, calm is a prerequisite

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:16.320
for resilience. When you feel like life

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:19.080
is weighing down on you or [music] you

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:21.480
are being swept away or thrashed around

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:23.840
by the currents of life, calm [music]

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:26.320
is what lets you find your stable

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:29.200
ground. And from that ground, you can

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:32.760
steady others as well. There's a

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:32.760
beautiful Sanskrit line.

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:39.153
